1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
Why send 170 units into the market
when the real imbalance is only 30?

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,500
Two verified institutions
want opposite trades.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:15,000
ILAL settles what cancels and sends
only the imbalance to Uniswap v4.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,500
The rails are already large:
roughly $4 trillion of Uniswap volume,

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:27,000
$12.7 trillion of adjusted stablecoin value
in the first half of 2025,

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,500
and more than $10 billion
in tokenized Treasuries.

7
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:38,500
Yet institutional controls still sit around the trade,
not inside final execution.

8
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,000
That is the entire why.
Now, the how.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
ILAL has two independent candidates today.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Session answers who may act:
prove compliance once through EAS or ZK,

11
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,500
and receive a short-lived grant.

12
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:01,500
SOEE answers how a group executes:
net opposite directions atomically,

13
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:05,000
and route only the imbalance to v4.
Today, I am demonstrating SOEE.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,500
An institution does not need to use
the Uniswap consumer interface.

15
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:16,500
Its treasury system connects through our SDK or CLI.
It defines the order, signs locally, and keeps custody.

16
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:21,500
A solver discovers eligible opposite flow
and selects orders for settlement.

17
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:30,000
But execution succeeds only if the Router and Hook
enforce every signed condition atomically.

18
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
This is the important boundary.

19
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:41,500
The institution signs the pool, direction, amount,
minimum output, maximum AMM input,

20
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:46,000
deadline, and nonce.

21
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,500
The solver may choose batch membership and timing,
but it cannot change those fields.

22
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:55,000
The Hook verifies and enforces them.
Authority to submit is not authority to decide.

23
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Institution A signs 100 units in one direction.
Institution B signs 70 in the other.

24
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Seventy matches seventy,
so 140 units of gross flow

25
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,500
settle without touching the AMM.

26
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:20,000
Only the 30-unit imbalance moves the Uniswap curve—
an 82.35% reduction in gross AMM exposure.

27
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,500
For the same fixed signed set, permutation cannot change
the batch ID, allocation, or pool result.

28
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:34,000
Policy, CNF, and the Chainlink circuit breaker
run before assets move.

29
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,500
The Hook nets opposing flow with before-swap deltas,
while v4 executes only one residual exact-input leg.

30
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The solver chooses membership and timing.
It never rewrites a signed order.

31
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The tradeoff is explicit.

32
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,500
The canonical case reduces AMM exposure by 82.35%
and improves aggregate output by 4.12 basis points—

33
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:59,500
but local execution gas is 3.57 times higher.

34
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:06,000
At one gwei, the modeled break-even trade value
ranges from about $1,387 to $2,774,

35
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
depending on the ETH price.
This is a reproducible benchmark, not a forecast.

36
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Now let's see the actual institutional workflow.

37
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:20,500
Institution A signs an order for 0.10 USDC.

38
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:27,500
Institution B signs the opposite order
for 0.07 hUSDT.

39
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:34,500
Both signatures remain local.
Nothing is broadcast yet.

40
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:41,500
ILAL matches 0.14 without touching the AMM
and routes only the 0.03 USDC imbalance to Uniswap v4.

41
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:49,500
After checking signatures, policy, credentials,
limits, and Chainlink,

42
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:54,500
the executor broadcasts one atomic transaction.

43
00:03:54,500 --> 00:04:00,000
It is confirmed on Base Sepolia.
Now let's verify it on BaseScan.

44
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
This is the successful Base Sepolia transaction.

45
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
The institutions submitted 0.17 tokens
of gross flow.

46
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
ILAL matched 0.14 without touching the AMM,
and only 0.03 reached Uniswap v4.

47
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Both nonces were consumed, every signed limit held,
and the Hook and Router finished with zero token inventory.

48
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
ILAL does not create another liquidity venue.

49
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,500
It provides a working path toward institution-ready
execution on existing Uniswap liquidity.

50
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:33,000
Session for access—EAS or ZK.
SOEE for execution—

51
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,500
net what cancels,
send only the residual.

52
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:40,000
This is an unaudited Base Sepolia candidate.
